\title{Odd numbers}
\date{2026-03-14T14:03:26Z}
\tag{discrete}
\taxon{Example}

\import{discrete-macros}

##{1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15, \dots}

\p{These are of course the odd numbers. The simplest way to describe them is to notice that each term is two more than the previous term, leading to the recurrence
##{\begin{align*}
    a_0 &= 1 \\
    a_n &= a_{n-1} + 2
\end{align*}}
Alternatively, we can describe them by the closed form #{a_n = 2n+1} (if we start at #{a_0}) or #{a_n = 2n-1} (if we start at #{a_1}).}

